# Frontend surfaces

Before changing surface behavior or layout, identify which runtime, route, shell, and viewport conditions actually own the affected experience.

Do not infer product surface from viewport alone. Native remains Native at large viewport; Web desktop may intentionally differ in layout, navigation, density, or interaction.

Keep shared product behavior and feature logic at their existing common owner. Split only presentation or interaction that genuinely differs by surface.

Do not duplicate a feature for Web desktop and Native/mobile when its responsibility remains shared, and do not force sharing through components filled with runtime or breakpoint branches.

Keep surface divergence at clear shell, navigation, runtime, or presentation boundaries rather than scattering `isNative`, desktop, or breakpoint decisions through feature trees.

Public or marketing surfaces follow their own layout ownership; do not impose application-shell or Native rules on them.

When behavior depends on Capacitor, OS lifecycle, keyboard, safe areas, deep links, system back, secure storage, or another Native capability, use `native-runtime-debug` rather than treating it as a generic responsive issue.